  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1255 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThe latest guidance continues to suggest quite an active \npattern across much of the Midwest, including Iowa, from this \nweekend into the middle of next week. There will be a risk for \nstrong to severe storms at times along with heavy rainfall. \n\nSaturday...\nLingering showers and storms along the cold front are possible \non Saturday morning, mainly across far southern Iowa. Severe \nweather is not expected. Depending on the location of the sfc \nboundary, storm re-development is possible again Saturday \nafternoon/evening. Most of the 12Z guidance suggests the front \nwill push into Missouri on Saturday afternoon, with the \nstrongest sfc based instability confined to the south. However, \na few deterministic models /e.g. NAM/ do show elevated \ninstability values over 1000 J/kg developing Saturday afternoon,\nso there remains a conditional risk for strong to severe \nstorms. Additional elevated storms are possible Saturday night\ninto early Sunday, but there is low confidence on the\norientation and strength of the low level jet.  \n\nSunday into Wednesday...\n\nPeriods of showers and thunderstorms are likely on Sunday into\nWednesday as a series of shortwaves move across the area. In \naddition, a strong LLJ is expected remain very active during the\nperiod, most notably on Sunday night and Monday and again on \nTuesday night. While mesoscale details are likely play a large \nrole in the convective evolution each day, the deterministic \nmodels/ensembles along with various NCAR AI guidance and the \nECMWF Extreme Forecast Index remain consistent in showing both \nthe potential for severe weather /Monday/ and heavy rainfall \nfrom Sunday through Wednesday. Correspondingly, the SPC \ncurrently has a Day 5 Slight Risk for severe storms and the WPC \nhas noted multiple days of Marginal/Slight via the Excessive \nRainfall Outlooks (EROs). Again, its too early to get into \nspecific details during this period but 7-day rainfall amounts \nin excess of 3-4\" are certainly possible with GEFS showing \nprobability of 2\" > 50%, and a 10-20% of greater than 4+\". This\ncould lead to areas of areal or river flooding, especially if\nthe higher end of the rainfall distribution occurs.  More to \ncome on this in future days.",
